# Peptide lab reports, analyzed

{{ included | comma }} published lab reports for {{ cov.included_compounds }} peptides and blends, aggregated vendor-blind by compound, laboratory and month, and released monthly with the method behind every number. This is release **{{ m.version }}**, built from records observed through {{ m.observed_through[:10] }}.

The per-record evidence, with the laboratory, client and batch as printed on each public report, is searchable at [titratelab.com/coa](https://titratelab.com/coa?src=github). This repository holds what can be said about the corpus as a whole.

## What the data shows

1. **Purity is rarely the problem. Quantity is.** Across the {{ q_rows | length }} compounds with at least 100 quantity results, the share of vials within 10% of the printed label runs from {{ q_worst.quantity_within_10pct_pct | pct1 }} ({{ q_worst.compound }}) to {{ q_best.quantity_within_10pct_pct | pct1 }} ({{ q_best.compound }}). Overall, {{ within_share | pct1 }} of {{ quantity_results | comma }} quantity results are within 10% of label and {{ under_label_total | comma }} are more than 10% under it. [Analysis](analysis/01-quantity-vs-label.md)
2. **Endotoxin testing is {% if endotoxin_share < 25 %}uncommon{% elif endotoxin_share < 50 %}a minority practice{% else %}common{% endif %}.** {{ endotoxin_share | pct1 }} of included reports carry any endotoxin result. A report without one says nothing about endotoxin either way. [Analysis](analysis/02-endotoxin-testing-rate.md)
3. **A small share of vials contain none of the labelled compound.** {{ zero_share | pct2 }} of purity results are reported at zero, meaning the laboratory did not identify the target compound. The rest cluster near the top: the median purity for most compounds is above 99%. [Analysis](analysis/03-purity-and-zero-identity.md)

